===Etymology===
From {{inh|en|enm|tapiestre}}, from {{der|en|fro|tapisserie||tapestry}}, from {{der|en|grc|τάπης}}, from an {{der|en|ira}} source.

===Pronunciation===
* {{enPR|tă'pəstrē}}, {{IPA|en|/ˈtæpəstɹi/}}
* {{audio|en|en-us-tapestry.ogg|a=US}}

[[Image:Tapestry_Room_from_Croome_Court_MET_DP341283.jpg|thumb|Tapestry Room from Croome Court.]]

===Noun===
{{en-noun|~}}

# A [[heavy]] [[woven]] [[cloth]], often with [[decorative]] [[pictorial]] [[design]]s, normally hung on walls.
#* {{RQ:Allingham China Governess|chapter=3|passage=Sepia Delft tiles surrounded the fireplace, their crudely drawn Biblical scenes in faded cyclamen blending with the pinkish pine, while above them, instead of a mantelshelf, there was an archway high enough to form a balcony with slender balusters and a '''tapestry'''-hung wall behind.}}
# {{lb|en|by extension}} Anything with [[variegated]] or [[complex]] [[detail]]s.
#* {{quote-journal|1=en|year=2013|month=January-February|author=Nancy Langston|title=The Fraught History of a Watery World|volume=101|issue=1|page=59|magazine=w:American Scientist|url=http://www.americanscientist.org/bookshelf/pub/the-fraught-history-of-a-watery-world|passage=European adventurers found themselves within a watery world, a '''tapestry''' of streams, channels, wetlands, lakes and lush riparian meadows enriched by floodwaters from the Mississippi River.|accessdate=9 May 2013|archiveurl=https://web.archive.org/web/20130122222520/http://www.americanscientist.org/bookshelf/pub/the-fraught-history-of-a-watery-world|archivedate=22 January 2013}}

